{
  "key": "E021",
  "title": "Heat-reuse economics with an approach-dependent dry cooler",
  "research_question": "When the dry cooler's fan power depends on approach temperature, when does warmer coolant reduce cooling electricity, how much chip leakage does it add, how much reusable heat does it create, and what does a supply floor cost per kWh of reusable heat compared with a heat pump?",
  "hypothesis": "Conditions: 2 parameter worlds (registry; RTX 5070-derived, as E012) x 4 plants (legacy; NTU dry cooler with fan_ratio 0.02 / 0.04 / 0.01) x 4 supply floors (none = 12 C, 35, 40, 44 C; t_sup_max is 45 C) x 3 ambients (8, 20, 30 C); S1 load profile, 4 h, instant plant, joint_fb; 5 seeds. Reusable heat = return water >= 45 C (t_reuse_min). H1 (fixing the plant moves the optimum): without a floor, joint_fb's mean supply with the NTU plant (fan 0.02) is at least 1 K warmer than with the legacy plant in all 6 world x ambient conditions. H2 (but not to reuse temperatures): without a floor, the NTU plant's (fan 0.02) mean supply stays below 35 C at 8 and 20 C ambient in both worlds. H3 (real silicon tolerates warmth better): at 8 and 20 C ambient the warming in H1 is larger in the RTX 5070 world than in the registry world. H4 (the floor gets cheaper): at 8 and 20 C ambient, in both worlds, the 40 C floor's energy cost (energy per successful task, floor vs none, paired) is lower with the NTU plant (fan 0.02) than with the legacy plant. H5 (fan cost sets the optimum): without a floor, at 8 and 20 C ambient in both worlds, mean supply orders as fan 0.04 >= fan 0.02 >= fan 0.01 (NTU). H6 (direct reuse is cheap per kWh): in the RTX 5070 world, with the NTU plant (fan 0.02) and with the legacy plant, the 44 C floor's extra grid electricity per kWh of reusable heat gained is below 0.10 kWh_el/kWh_th at 8 and 20 C ambient. H7 (warm loop beats a heat pump): in the same conditions as H6, that ratio is lower than the electricity a heat pump would need to lift the no-floor return water to 45 C + 2 K (heat_pump.upgrade with eta_II 0.5, 3 K approaches; evaluated at the run's mean return temperature). Descriptive: break-even heat-to-electricity price ratios per condition, and sensitivity to fan cost.",
  "null_hypothesis": "With an approach-dependent dry cooler, the energy-optimal supply and the cost of warm-water reuse are the same as with the legacy plant.",
  "independent_variables": [
    "world (2)",
    "plant (4)",
    "supply floor (4)",
    "ambient (3)"
  ],
  "dependent_variables": [
    "T_sup_mean_C",
    "T_ret_mean_C",
    "E_per_success_kJ",
    "E_grid_kWh",
    "E_it_kWh",
    "E_fan_kWh",
    "E_chiller_kWh",
    "E_pump_kWh",
    "Q_reusable_kWh",
    "Q_rejected_kWh"
  ],
  "controlled_variables": [
    "S1 load profile (mean 0.5, amplitude 0.25)",
    "seeds 0-4",
    "warm start",
    "instant plant",
    "policy joint_fb",
    "t_reuse_min 45 C"
  ],
  "baseline": "legacy plant within each world, floor and ambient; no floor within each world, plant and ambient",
  "configuration": "Worlds {'registry': {}, 'rtx5070': {'beta_compute': 0.983, 'beta_memory': 0.0, 'kappa_leak': 0.02, 'p_leak_ref': 27.42, 'v_min_ratio': 0.78, 'vf_knee': 0.805}}. Plants {'legacy': {'plant_model': 0}, 'ntu': {'plant_model': 1}, 'ntu_fan_hi': {'plant_model': 1, 'fan_ratio': 0.04}, 'ntu_fan_lo': {'plant_model': 1, 'fan_ratio': 0.01}}. Floors {'none': 12.0, 'f35': 35.0, 'f40': 40.0, 'f44': 44.0}. Ambient [8.0, 20.0, 30.0].",
  "evaluation_criteria": "Mean supply as seed means; energy as paired relative differences with 95% t-CI; ratios from seed-mean energies and heats.",
  "sample_size": "5 seeds (paired), 480 runs.",
  "randomization": "Workloads drawn per seed and shared across all variants.",
  "measurement_boundary": "Simulated facility electricity (IT, conversion, pump, fans, chiller) of one node; reusable heat is heat leaving at >= 45 C, assumed fully demanded (an upper bound on its value); heat-pump electricity is computed in analysis, not simulated; no heat-customer dynamics.",
  "known_limitations": "NTU dry-cooler parameters are class F; the fan-ratio variants are a sensitivity, not a calibration. Constant ambient per run, no seasonal heat demand, no heat price model beyond a ratio. Heat-pump comparison uses mean return temperature. Simulation only.",
  "disclosure": "Known before registration: E012's results and audit; the NTU dry cooler's fan power at the node's design heat (100 W at 25 C supply and 20 C ambient, ~14 W at 30 C, ~4 W at 35 C); a back-of-envelope estimate (PHASE_REPORT, E012) that with a cubic fan law the optimum approach would be ~6-8 K. One 30-minute S1 run with plant_model 1 (unit test: fan energy lower than legacy). No E021 cell was run before registration.",
